1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:02,680
place.

2
00:00:02,720 --> 00:00:08,720
Their most faithful disciples were the two cart horses, Boxer and Clover.

3
00:00:08,720 --> 00:00:13,279
These two had great difficulty in thinking anything out for themselves.

4
00:00:13,279 --> 00:00:17,480
But having once accepted the pigs as their teachers, they absorbed everything

5
00:00:17,480 --> 00:00:23,240
that they were told and passed it on to the other animals by simple arguments.

6
00:00:23,240 --> 00:00:27,920
They were unfailing in their attendance at the secret meetings in the barn and

7
00:00:27,920 --> 00:00:34,119
led the singing of Beasts of England with which the meetings always ended.

8
00:00:34,119 --> 00:00:39,599
Now, as it turned out, the rebellion was achieved much earlier and more easily

9
00:00:39,599 --> 00:00:42,200
than anyone had expected.

10
00:00:42,200 --> 00:00:48,599
In past years, Mr. Jones, although a hard master, had been a capable farmer.

11
00:00:48,599 --> 00:00:52,400
But of late, he had fallen on evil days.

12
00:00:52,400 --> 00:00:57,039
He had become much disheartened after losing money in a lawsuit and had taken

13
00:00:57,039 --> 00:01:00,200
to drinking more than was good for him.

14
00:01:00,200 --> 00:01:04,599
For whole days at a time, he would lounge in his Windsor chair in the

15
00:01:04,599 --> 00:01:10,240
kitchen, reading the newspapers, drinking, and occasionally feeding Moses

16
00:01:10,240 --> 00:01:13,920
on crusts of bread soaked in beer.

17
00:01:13,920 --> 00:01:17,079
His men were idle and dishonest.

18
00:01:17,079 --> 00:01:21,640
The fields were full of weeds. The buildings wanted roofing.

19
00:01:21,640 --> 00:01:26,759
The hedges were neglected. And the animals were underfed.

20
00:01:26,759 --> 00:01:30,920
June came and the hay was almost ready for cutting.

21
00:01:30,920 --> 00:01:35,680
On Midsummer's Eve, which was a Saturday, Mr. Jones went into Willingdon

22
00:01:35,680 --> 00:01:43,079
and got so drunk at the Red Lion that he did not come back till midday on Sunday.

23
00:01:43,079 --> 00:01:47,920
The men had milked the cows in the early morning and then had gone out rabbiting

24
00:01:47,920 --> 00:01:50,880
without bothering to feed the animals.

25
00:01:50,880 --> 00:01:55,920
When Mr. Jones got back, he immediately went to sleep on the drawing-room sofa

26
00:01:55,920 --> 00:02:01,400
with the News of the World over his face so that when evening came, the animals

27
00:02:01,400 --> 00:02:04,039
were still unfed.

28
00:02:04,039 --> 00:02:07,799
At last, they could stand it no longer.

29
00:02:07,799 --> 00:02:12,440
One of the cows broke in the door of the store-shed with her horn and all the

30
00:02:12,440 --> 00:02:16,079
animals began to help themselves from the bins.

31
00:02:16,079 --> 00:02:20,039
It was just then that Mr. Jones woke up.

32
00:02:20,039 --> 00:02:25,480
The next moment, he and his four men were in the store-shed with whips in

33
00:02:25,480 --> 00:02:29,960
their hands lashing out in all directions.

34
00:02:29,960 --> 00:02:33,759
This was more than the hungry animals could bear.

35
00:02:33,759 --> 00:02:38,519
With one accord, though nothing of the kind had been planned beforehand, they

36
00:02:38,519 --> 00:02:42,240
flung themselves upon their tormentors.

37
00:02:42,240 --> 00:02:49,200
Jones and his men suddenly found themselves being butted and kicked from all sides.

38
00:02:49,200 --> 00:02:52,559
The situation was quite out of their control.

39
00:02:52,559 --> 00:02:57,599
They had never seen animals behave like this before and this sudden uprising of

40
00:02:57,599 --> 00:03:02,079
creatures whom they were used to thrashing and maltreating just as they

41
00:03:02,079 --> 00:03:06,960
chose frightened them almost out of their wits.

42
00:03:06,960 --> 00:03:13,640
After only a moment or two, they gave up trying to defend themselves and took to their heels.

43
00:03:13,640 --> 00:03:19,279
A minute later, all five of them were in full flight down the cart-track that led

44
00:03:19,279 --> 00:03:24,480
to the main road with the animals pursuing them in triumph.

45
00:03:24,480 --> 00:03:28,440
Mrs. Jones looked out of the bedroom window, saw what was happening,

46
00:03:28,440 --> 00:03:35,160
hurriedly flung a few possessions into a carpet bag and slipped out of the farm by another way.

47
00:03:35,160 --> 00:03:40,000
Moses sprang off his perch and flapped after her, croaking loudly.

48
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:44,640
Meanwhile, the animals had chased Jones and his men out onto the road and

49
00:03:44,640 --> 00:03:48,000
slammed the five-barred gate behind them.

50
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:52,559
And so, almost before they knew what was happening, the rebellion had been

51
00:03:52,559 --> 00:03:54,880
successfully carried through.

52
00:03:54,880 --> 00:03:59,960
Jones was expelled, and the Manor Farm was theirs.

53
00:03:59,960 --> 00:04:05,319
For the first few minutes, the animals could hardly believe in their good fortune.

54
00:04:05,319 --> 00:04:10,640
Their first act was to gallop in a body right round the boundaries of the farm,

55
00:04:10,640 --> 00:04:16,720
as though to make quite sure that no human being was hiding anywhere upon it.

56
00:04:16,720 --> 00:04:25,440
Then, they raced back to the farm buildings to wipe out the last traces of Jones's hated reign.

57
00:04:25,440 --> 00:04:31,000
The harness room at the end of the stables was broken open. The bits, the

58
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:36,640
nose rings, the dog chains, the cruel knives with which Mr. Jones had been

59
00:04:36,640 --> 00:04:42,640
used to castrate the pigs and lambs were all flung down the well.

60
00:04:42,640 --> 00:04:49,079
The reins, the halters, the blinkers, the degrading nose bags were thrown onto

61
00:04:49,079 --> 00:04:52,359
the rubbish fire, which was burning in the yard.

62
00:04:52,359 --> 00:04:54,440
So were the whips.

63
00:04:54,440 --> 00:05:00,400
All the animals capered with joy when they saw the whips going up in flames.

64
00:05:00,400 --> 00:05:05,960
Snowball also threw onto the fire the ribbons with which the horses' manes and

65
00:05:05,960 --> 00:05:10,240
tails had usually been decorated on market days.

66
00:05:10,240 --> 00:05:16,039
>> "Ribbons should be considered as clothes, which are the mark of a human being.

67
00:05:16,039 --> 00:05:19,400
All animals should go naked."

68
00:05:19,400 --> 00:05:23,720
>> When Boxer heard this, he fetched the small straw hat which he wore in summer

69
00:05:23,720 --> 00:05:29,160
to keep the flies out of his ears and flung it onto the fire with the rest.

70
00:05:29,160 --> 00:05:35,920
In a very little while, the animals had destroyed everything that reminded them of Mr. Jones.

71
00:05:35,920 --> 00:05:40,200
Napoleon then led them back to the store-shed and served out a double

72
00:05:40,200 --> 00:05:45,880
ration of corn to everybody, with two biscuits for each dog.

73
00:05:45,880 --> 00:05:53,160
Then they sang Beasts of England from end to end seven times running.

74
00:05:53,160 --> 00:06:00,000
And after that, they settled down for the night and slept as they had never slept before.
